[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The Location C L3 gateway and upstream routing are healthy. From a client in Location C (pc11), the default gateway 192.168.200.1 responds normally (2/2 ICMP replies, ~0.6 ms). Router4, which serves as Location C’s gateway, has eth0 up at 192.168.200.1/24 and is forwarding upstream via eth5 (172.25.1.1) with a default route to 172.25.1.2. Its routing table contains multiple RIP-learned prefixes (proto rip), confirming the routing daemon is functioning and adjacencies are established. Moreover, Location C hosts can reach other sites: pc11 successfully pinged pc4 (172.16.10.4) with 0% loss and ~1.6 ms, and the topology-wide check shows pc11–pc14 all reach remote pc5 (172.16.10.5) and each other with 0% loss.

Mechanism: If the gateway or site router were “dead,” ARP and ICMP to 192.168.200.1 would fail and no intersite pings would succeed. Instead, we observe normal ARP/ICMP to the gateway and working intersite paths, so the reported “cut off” symptom does not reproduce.

Localization: Router4 eth0 (192.168.200.1) is up; Router4 eth4 (192.168.15.2) and eth5 (172.25.1.1) are up; RIP is active on Router4. Client routes on pc11–pc14 point correctly to 192.168.200.1.

Root cause: No network fault found; the outage was not present during testing and is likely host-specific or transient.

Corrective fix: No restart needed. Advise affected users to check local host issues (e.g., firewall/DNS), and if problems recur, capture traceroute/ARP table when failing to isolate any client-side cause. We will continue to monitor. 
=== END ===